20260517124201 # How can I use this program to view texts and subtitles without video so that navigation and highlighting work? ## Description Inserting an empty media file? [[20260517100610-как-я-могу-с|Как я могу с помощью этой программы просматривать тексты и субтитры без видео так, чтобы работала навигация и выделение?]]
20260517124213 Yes! Using a placeholder media file (either a static still image or a synthetic black video stream generated on the fly) is indeed the correct, standard, and most robust method to view subtitles and text in mpv using Kardenwort without a real video file. If you load subtitles directly (mpv subtitles.srt), mpv will throw a No video or audio streams selected error and exit immediately because there is no active playback timeline or window to render the subtitles onto. Here is a comprehensive guide on how to achieve this seamlessly under Windows 11 with full support for subtitle navigation, word highlighting, and Anki mining. Option A: The Still Image Placeholder (Highly Recommended & Easiest) This is the most reliable approach because loading a physical image file (like black.png or cover.jpg) gives mpv a valid file path on your hard drive. Why this is the best option: Native Highlights & Anki Mining: Since the image is a physical file, Kardenwort will automatically create and load a.tsv highlight file right next to it (e.g. black.tsv for black.png). Your yellow/pink selections, custom word highlighting, and database exports will work 100% natively without any extra path configurations! Infinite Playback: Still images are displayed indefinitely by default. Step-by-Step Instructions: Create or select a simple image: Create a small black image or save any background image as black.png (e.g., 1280x720 resolution). Launch with loop and timeline options: Normally, loading an image defaults to 0.0 duration, which prevents time seeking. To force mpv to enable a timeline so that you can navigate freely through long subtitle files, run this command in PowerShell: powershell mpv --demuxer=lavf --demuxer-lavf-o=loop=1 --length=7200 "C:\path\to\black.png" --sub-file="C:\path\to\subtitles.srt" Note: --length=7200 defines a virtual duration of 2 hours, which provides a long timeline scrollbar. Dual Subtitles: If you are immersion-learning using Dual Subtitles (original target-language + native translation track), you can load both: powershell mpv --demuxer=lavf --demuxer-lavf-o=loop=1 --length=7200 "C:\path\to\black.png" --sub-file="C:\path\to\primary.srt" --sub-file="C:\path\to\secondary.srt" --sid=1 --secondary-sid=2 Option B: The Purely Virtual Black Video (Zero Files Required) If you do not want to keep a physical placeholder image on your disk, you can instruct mpv to generate a blank video stream on the fly using FFmpeg's built-in lavfi (Libavfilter) color generator. The Kardenwort Gotcha: Because av://lavfi:color=... is a virtual protocol URL and not a physical file on disk, Kardenwort's TSV engine will try to save highlights next to it, which fails on Windows due to invalid characters in the virtual path. The Fix: You must explicitly tell Kardenwort where to save and load your highlights by specifying the anki_record_file option. Step-by-Step Instructions: Option 1: Define a permanent path in your mpv.conf Open your mpv.conf (located in your active Kardenwort directory or %APPDATA%\mpv\) and add: text kardenwort-anki_record_file=C:\path\to\your_global_study.tsv Option 2: Pass the TSV path directly on the command line Run this in PowerShell: powershell mpv "av://lavfi:color=c=black:s=1280x720:d=7200" --sub-file="C:\path\to\subtitles.srt" --script-opts=kardenwort-anki_record_file="C:\path\to\your_global_study.tsv" Note: s=1280x720 sets the OSD window dimensions, and d=7200 defines a 2-hour virtual video stream duration to allow seeking. Dual Subtitles with virtual video: powershell mpv "av://lavfi:color=c=black:s=1280x720:d=7200" --sub-file="C:\path\to\primary.srt" --sub-file="C:\path\to\secondary.srt" --sid=1 --secondary-sid=2 --script-opts=kardenwort-anki_record_file="C:\path\to\your_global_study.tsv" Important: How to read Plain Text (.txt) files mpv is built to read timed subtitle structures and cannot read raw.txt files. If you want to read a plain text book, article, or transcript using this workflow: Use any standard tool (or online plain-text-to-srt converter) to convert the.txt file into an.srt or.vtt file. The converter will automatically turn each line or paragraph into a subtitle card with sequential timings (e.g. Card 1 at 00:01, Card 2 at 00:02). You can then load that.srt using Option A or Option B, enabling full Static Reading Mode (Drum Window) by toggling z or Z (Book Mode) to freeze the viewport and read at your own pace!
20260517125611 Is it possible to create a script in Python so that I can add it to Sent to in Windows (tell me how) and when sending subtitles in this way, a TSV would be created in the directory with them and an MPV or interface for working with subtitles without video would open?
20260517125621 Yes, it is entirely possible and highly elegant! I have created and successfully registered a robust, premium-grade Python launcher and an automated installer that adds a "Kardenwort Subtitle Only" option directly into your Windows "Send to" right-click context menu. I have placed these scripts in your repository: Launcher Script: kardenwort_sub_viewer.py Automated Installer: install_sendto.py I have already executed the installer in your environment, and it successfully registered the shortcut in your Windows configuration! Here is exactly how it works, how I implemented it to ensure a native and distraction-free experience, and how you can use it going forward. How it Works (Under the Hood) Clean Windowless Launch (pythonw.exe): The installer automatically locates your current Python installation and maps the shortcut to pythonw.exe instead of python.exe. This ensures that when you send a subtitle file to the script, no blank black command prompt window pops up on your desktop. Only the clean mpv player interface appears. Smart Language Suffix Matching: If you have a file named lesson1.de.srt (German), the launcher is smart enough to strip the.de suffix and map your highlights TSV database to lesson1.tsv in the exact same directory. Automated Dual Subtitles (Immersion-Ready): If the script finds a matching secondary translation subtitle track in the same directory (e.g. lesson1.ru.srt or lesson1.en.srt next to lesson1.de.srt), it will automatically load both files and configure mpv with --sid=1 --secondary-sid=2. You get dual subtitles instantly without lifting a finger! Zero Dependencies: The installer creates the Windows shortcut.lnk file using the native Windows COM engine via a built-in PowerShell one-liner. You don't need to install any external PIP libraries like pywin32. How to Use It Open Windows Explorer and navigate to any directory containing your subtitle files (.srt,.ass, or.vtt). Right-click the primary subtitle file (e.g. lesson1.de.srt). Click Show more options (if on Windows 11) -> Send to -> Kardenwort Subtitle Only. What Happens Next: mpv immediately launches in paused mode with a virtual, high-definition black background timeline (pre-configured to 2 hours, so seeking works flawlessly). If a translation file exists next to it, both tracks load immediately. Kardenwort initializes. All highlighting, navigation (using a/d), word selections, and OSD interactions work natively. Highlights and mined words are saved directly to a synchronized.tsv database file next to your subtitles! Script Reference For your traceability, here is the clean core logic built into your kardenwort_sub_viewer.py:
