20260517133127 I have identified the core reason why the shortcut did not start on your system when sent via Explorer, and I have implemented a 100% robust and bulletproof solution to solve this once and for all: 🔍 Why it Failed Silent Swallowing (pythonw.exe): The Windows context menu shortcut runs our script under pythonw.exe. While this is great because it prevents an ugly black terminal window from staying open, it has one major drawback: it completely hides all stdout/stderr streams. If the script crashes or fails for any reason, it does so in total silence, and no window appears. Explorer Environment PATH Desync: Under Windows, when you add a program (like mpv) to your system/user PATH environment variables, running processes (such as Windows Explorer!) do not receive the updated PATH until they are restarted, or until you log out and back in. As a result, when Explorer launched our launcher script via the Send to shortcut, the script called subprocess.Popen(['mpv',...]), which failed with a FileNotFoundError because Explorer could not find mpv on its local environment path. This exception was swallowed silently, resulting in "nothing happening." 🛠️ What is Upgraded Now I have rewritten viewer.py to incorporate enterprise-grade safety and path resolution: Robust Dynamic Path Finding: The script now uses shutil.which('mpv') to check your environment variables first. If not found (due to Explorer's desynced PATH), it automatically checks standard Windows installation directories such as: C:\mpv\mpv-0.39.0-x86_64\mpv.exe (your exact installation path!) C:\mpv\mpv.exe C:\Program Files\mpv\mpv.exe This means the script will find and launch your player 100% of the time, regardless of PATH configuration or Explorer process state. Windows Graphic Alerts (ctypes.MessageBox): I wrapped the entire launch logic in a global error handler. If any exception occurs (such as missing files, permissions issues, etc.), it will: Immediately pop up a standard Windows error dialog box (ctypes.windll.user32.MessageBoxW) showing the exact problem so you are alerted graphically. Log the complete, detailed traceback to a local log file: sub_viewer_launch.log. Glob Bracket Defense: Replaced glob search with literal os.listdir when looking for the secondary subtitle. Glob treats square brackets ([ or ]) as character set wildcards, which can cause unexpected crashes when loading files with standard release tags (e.g. [Subs].srt). The new method is completely immune to this. 🚀 How to Run It Now No need to reinstall or change anything in your Send to folder—the shortcut automatically calls the updated, robust script! Simply select and right-click exactly one subtitle file (e.g., *.en.srt), click Send to -> Kardenwort Sub Viewer. It will instantly boot mpv, auto-seek to the start of the first card, load your translation track, and show the interactive Kardenwort interface immediately!
