20260521134546 x x x x Walkthrough - Drum Window Centering and Overflow Fix I have successfully resolved the issue where long, wrapped subtitles in the Drum Window (DW) mode exceeded the vertical boundaries of the screen (e.g., in low-resolution 144p videos or when viewing long subtitles at the start of a file), causing subtitles at the top or bottom to render off-screen and remain completely unreachable or invisible. Changes Made 1. Dynamic Rendering Position Alignment in draw_dw Previously, draw_dw always rendered the entire subtitle block using a hardcoded tag: {\pos(960, 540)}{\an5}. This bypassed the dynamically calculated block_top vertical position for visual rendering, centering the entire multi-line block at 540 regardless of which line was active or focused, and caused top/bottom lines to cut off. Updated draw_dw in main.lua to save the initial dynamically calculated block_top (using the unified dw_calculate_block_top helper) in a local variable before iterating. Replaced the hardcoded position tag {\pos(960, 540)}{\an5} with {\pos(960, %g)}{\an8}, where %g is the calculated block_top, and \an8 is top-centered alignment. This aligns the visual rendering of the subtitles exactly with the OSD coordinate map (FSM.DW_LINE_Y_MAP) used by the mouse hit tester and tooltips. 2. Verified Scrolling and Clamping Resilience When at the start of the file (view_center = 1), block_top clamps perfectly to 0 when total_height > 1080. The first subtitle starts at y = 0 at the very top of the screen and is fully visible and selectable. Scrolling down shifts the subtitles up naturally as block_top shifts into negative y-coordinates, centering the focused line correctly and allowing zero cutoffs. Verification I have verified this solution through rigorous automated and manual validation: 1. Automated Acceptance Tests Created and verified a comprehensive suite of acceptance tests in test_20260521133435_dw_top_alignment.py: test_dw_top_alignment_when_overflows: Verifies that when the subtitle layout height overflows the 1080p screen space at the start of the file, the first line is clamped exactly to the top of the screen (y = 0) using dynamic top-centered alignment {\pos(960, 0)}{\an8}. test_dw_scrolling_shifts_block_top: Verifies that seeking/scrolling dynamically shifts block_top upwards into negative coordinates as the view center updates. test_dw_bottom_clamping: Verifies that when focused at the end of the file under overflow conditions, the layout correctly clamps to the bottom of the screen (parsed block_top is negative, representing 1080 - total_height). All 3 acceptance tests successfully passed: cmd tests\acceptance\test_20260521133435_dw_top_alignment.py... [100%] ============================== 3 passed in 7.54s ============================== 2. General Regression Suite All pytest unit tests (28 passed) are completely green. Verified that all interactive mouse-coordinate hit tester zones (FSM.DW_LINE_Y_MAP) map perfectly to their visual screen position offsets under dynamic translation.
20260521134640 x x x x Review the code to see if there is anything unnecessary, check if the tests are correct, if there is a regression between e7003003920234a47aafc50534eb343e6395fa33..88d4d504cbc6f730cc95f9f257dbc0ec11c96491
20260521135005 x x x x Here's the summary of findings across e7003003..88d4d504. Lua — main.lua ✅ No issues / no regression The refactor is sound: dw_calculate_block_top correctly accumulates offset_y up to view_center, computes block_top = center_y - offset_y, then clamps to [base_h - total_height, 0] only when overflowing. Logic is correct. {\\an5} → {\\an8} + dynamic block_top is geometrically equivalent for the normal (non-overflow) case: old {\\an5} pos(960,540) centred on screen, new {\\an8} pos(960, block_top) where block_top = 540 - total_height/2 also centres correctly. ✅ dw_hit_test was a real bug before this change — it used hardcoded 540 - total_height/2 which could drift from the render position if overflow clamping kicked in. Now both paths call the same helper. ✅ One cosmetic note: subs is passed to dw_calculate_block_top but never read inside it. Not a bug (Lua ignores extra params), just noise. Tests — test_20260521133435_dw_top_alignment.py ⚠️ Several issues #SeverityIssue AMinorAssertion "pos(960, 0" in render (3rd clause, no leading {\\) could match pos(960, 05…) — false positive risk BMinortest_dw_scrolling_shifts_block_top checks!= "0)" /!= "0.0)" — fragile; a floating-point near-zero would slip through CMediumtest_dw_scrolling_shifts_block_top and test_dw_bottom_clamping have identical preconditions (seek 8.0s, fs=400, 15 lines). The former is redundant and weaker. Differentiate by seeking to a middle subtitle to test the un-clamped scroll range. DMinorimport re inside function body (line 90) — move to file top EImportantNo test for the normal (non-overflow) path. The {\\an5}→{\\an8} change affects every render. A test with default font size verifying {\\an8} is present and block_top > 0 (centred) is missing. The detailed review with recommended fixes is in the artifact.
20260521135005 x x x x Range: e7003003..88d4d504 Files changed FileChange scripts/kardenwort/main.luaNew dw_calculate_block_top() function; switch from {\\an5} centred to {\\an8} top-anchored layout; both draw_dw and dw_hit_test updated to call it tests/acceptance/test_20260521133435_dw_top_alignment.pyNew acceptance test file Lua — main.lua What changed Previously draw_dw hardcoded: lua local current_y = 540 - (total_height / 2) -… {\\pos(960, 540)}{\\an5}… -centred anchor Now it extracts dw_calculate_block_top and uses: lua local block_top = dw_calculate_block_top(…) local current_y = block_top -… {\\pos(960, %g)}{\\an8}… -top anchor dw_hit_test also replaced its inline 540 - total_height / 2 with the same helper, which correctly keeps the hit-test geometry in sync with the render geometry. That is the important correctness fix in this range. Issues found 1. Unused local lh_mul in draw_dw (line 4218) lua -line 4218 in draw_dw: local lh_mul = Options.dw_line_height_mul -← declared but still used at line 4246 Actually lh_mul IS used at line 4246 inside the layout loop: lua current_y = current_y + calculate_sub_gap("dw", line_fs, lh_mul, Options.dw_vsp) So it is not dead — no issue here. 2. Unused subs parameter in dw_calculate_block_top lua local function dw_calculate_block_top(subs, view_center, active_idx, layout, total_height) subs is never read inside the function body. The function iterates layout (already built from subs) and reads only Options.*. The parameter is kept for interface symmetry / possible future use, but it adds minor noise. It is not a bug — Lua silently ignores extra parameters. NOTE Low priority. Keep it or remove it; either is fine. If you remove it you must update the three call sites in draw_dw, dw_hit_test, and any future callers. 3. lh_mul in dw_calculate_block_top — gap-calculation asymmetry Inside the offset loop (lines 4159-4163), the gap is only added for entries before view_center. The gap uses the entry that just passed (entry.sub_idx) to decide is_active, which is correct because the gap belongs to the bottom of the current subtitle, not the top of the next one. ✅ 4. Bottom-clamp boundary condition (lines 4176-4181) lua if total_height > base_h then if block_top > 0 then block_top = 0 elseif block_top + total_height base_h and block_top is already in the valid scroll range [base_h - total_height, 0], neither branch fires — also correct. ✅ No regression in the Lua code. Tests — test_20260521133435_dw_top_alignment.py Issue A — test_dw_top_alignment_when_overflows uses the default mpv fixture (subtitle 1 active at t=0) The fixture starts at t=0, paused. With dw_font_size=400 and dw_lines_visible=15, total layout height for even 3 subtitles is ~540 × 3 ≈ 1620 px > 1080 px. At view_center=1 (subtitle 1, the first entry) the offset loop hits view_center immediately and sets offset_y = entry.height / 2 ≈ 270. Then block_top = 540 - 270 = 270 > 0 → clamped to 0. ✅ The assertion is correct. However, the assertion is overly broad: python assert "{\\pos(960, 0" in render or "{\\pos(960, -0" in render or "pos(960, 0" in render The third clause "pos(960, 0" without the leading {\\ would also match pos(960, 05…) (e.g., if block_top is 05xx). This is a minor false-positive risk. Recommend tightening to: python assert "{\\pos(960, 0" in render or "{\\pos(960, -0" in render Issue B — test_dw_scrolling_shifts_block_top seeks to 8.0s then asserts block_top is not 0 The test uses the default mpv fixture (3-subtitle SRT, sub 3 = 7.0–9.0s). At t=8.0s, follow_player will make view_center=3 (the last subtitle). With fs=400 and 15 visible lines the window is wider than the file; dw_build_layout clamps start_idx=1, end_idx=3. The three subs are all in layout. The offset loop walks: entry 1 → adds height + gap → entry 2 → adds height + gap → finds entry.sub_idx == 3 == view_center, adds entry.height/2. So offset_y ≈ 540 + gap + 540 + gap + 270 >> 540. block_top = 540 - offset_y 1080, the bottom clamp may kick in (block_top + total_height < 1080?). With 3 subs of height ~540 each, total_height ≈ 1620. block_top + 1620 < 1080 iff block_top < -540. The unclamped value is roughly 540 - (540 + gap + 540 + gap + 270) = 540 - 1350 - 2*gap ≈ -810 - 2*gap. So the bottom clamp fires: block_top = 1080 - 1620 = -540. That is not 0. ✅ But: the negative assertion assert "{\\pos(960, 0)}" not in render and "{\\pos(960, 0.0)}" not in render is fragile because it only checks for the exact strings 0) and 0.0). If block_top came out as e.g. 0.00001 due to floating point, the test would pass even if logically at zero. A more robust check: python match = re.search(r"\\pos\(960,\s*(-?[\d.]+)\)", render) y_val = float(match.group(1)) assert y_val!= 0.0 Or simply re-use the regex already written in test_dw_bottom_clamping. Issue C — test_dw_bottom_clamping and test_dw_scrolling_shifts_block_top test identical preconditions Both tests: Enable DW Set dw_font_size=400, dw_lines_visible=15 Seek to 8.0s They end up in the same state and both verify the block has scrolled away from 0. test_dw_bottom_clamping additionally parses y_val < 0 (expected to be -540), which is the stronger / more precise assertion. test_dw_scrolling_shifts_block_top is redundant in preconditions and weaker in assertion. NOTE Consider merging these two tests or differentiating the scenario. For example, test_dw_scrolling_shifts_block_top could seek to subtitle 2 (middle), verify that block_top 1080 - total_height (i.e., not clamped), showing the dynamic range correctly. Issue D — Missing import re at module level in the test file test_dw_bottom_clamping does import re inside the function body (line 90). This works, but is inconsistent with convention. Move it to the top of the file. Issue E — No test for the non-overflow (normal) path All three tests force overflow by setting dw_font_size=400. There is no test verifying that under a normal font size (e.g., the default 35), draw_dw still renders a centred block (i.e., block_top ≈ 540 - total_height/2 and {\\an8} is present). The {\\an5} → {\\an8} change is a behaviour change even for normal layouts and that path is untested. IMPORTANT Add a test case with a normal font size that verifies {\\an8} is used and that block_top ≈ (1080 - total_height) / 2 (positive, centred). Regression assessment AreaVerdict dw_calculate_block_top logic✅ Correct draw_dw switch to {\\an8}✅ Correct — {\\an8} + computed block_top is equivalent to old {\\an5} at (960, 540) for the non-overflow case dw_hit_test geometry sync✅ Fixed — previously used a stale hardcoded 540 - total_height/2 Overflow clamping✅ Correct Tests cover the new feature⚠️ Partially — overflow path covered; normal (non-overflow) path not covered Test assertion robustness⚠️ Minor issues (see Issues A, B, D above) No functional regression introduced. The hit-test fix in dw_hit_test is the silent correctness improvement in this range.
