20260521141451 x x x x DW Click-Accuracy Drift — Root-Cause Analysis ZID: 20260521141451 Symptom In DW mode, clicking on words in lower subtitles becomes increasingly inaccurate. The further down a subtitle is in the list, the worse the drift. With 3-line wrapping, the error accumulates fast. DM mode does not exhibit this problem (or much less so). Root Cause The DW hit-test recomputes y-geometry independently from the render, using a calibration constant (dw_line_height_mul = 0.87) that doesn't perfectly match the ASS renderer's actual font line spacing. The per-line error accumulates over many visual lines. How DW renders vs how it hit-tests ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐ │ ASS RENDERER (libass) │ │ │ │ Each \N line break advances by: │ │ font_ascent + font_descent + vsp_override │ │ ≈ font_size * REAL_RATIO + vsp │ │ │ │ Where REAL_RATIO comes from the font's actual metrics │ │ (typically ~0.85-1.05 depending on font/size) │ └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘ ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐ │ DW HIT-TEST MODEL (dw_hit_test) │ │ │ │ Each visual line has height: │ │ vline_h = font_size * dw_line_height_mul + vsp │ │ = font_size * 0.87 + vsp │ │ │ │ Where 0.87 is a CALIBRATION CONSTANT that approximates │ │ the real font metrics │ └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘ Per-line error If the font's real line ratio is R and the calibration is 0.87: error_per_line = font_size × (R − 0.87) + 0 For typical fonts where R ≈ 0.92: error_per_line = 34 × 0.05 = 1.7 px Accumulated drift With 7 visible subtitles × 3 wrapped lines each = 21 visual lines: total_drift ≈ 21 × 1.7 = ~36 px ≈ 1 full visual line height This means by the 7th subtitle at the bottom of the DW, the hit-test is targeting the wrong visual line — off by an entire row. The user experiences this as clicking on one word but selecting the word above or below. Inter-subtitle gap also drifts The \\N\\N double-gap separator adds further error because the ASS engine renders two newlines at font_real_height + vsp_adjusted each, while the model uses calculate_sub_gap which may produce a different total. Each subtitle boundary contributes an additional delta. Why DM Is More Accurate DM (draw_drum / drum_osd_hit_test) has the same fundamental model imprecision — it also uses lh_mul to compute heights. But two factors mask it: FactorDMDW Visible lines~3-5 subtitles × 1-2 lines = ~6 lines7-15 subtitles × 1-3 lines = ~21-45 lines Cumulative drift6 × 1.7 ≈ 10 px21 × 1.7 ≈ 36 px Snap threshold60 px generous snapStrict vline_h division Hit architectureHit zones built during render (shared cur_y)Hit-test recalculates geometry from scratch Even though DM's model also drifts from ASS reality, the small number of lines keeps the total error within the 60px snap threshold. DW has too many lines for the error to stay invisible. Fix Strategies Option A: Build DW hit zones during draw_dw (like DM) Populate a DW_HIT_ZONES array inside draw_dw's existing layout loop (lines 4239-4282) — the same loop that already computes current_y and FSM.DW_LINE_Y_MAP. Each visual line gets an entry with y_top, y_bottom, x_start, total_width, and word positions. Then replace dw_hit_test's independent y-geometry with a simple zone lookup (like drum_osd_hit_test). Pros: Render and hit-test share the exact same y-accumulation — they can never drift from each other Eliminates the independent sub_gap calculation in dw_hit_test Follows the proven DM pattern Cons: Both will still drift from ASS reality (the model error remains), but at least they drift together The dw_line_height_mul calibration still matters for overall layout feel Option B: Calibrate dw_line_height_mul more precisely Measure the actual font line height at runtime and set lh_mul dynamically. Pros: Fixes the root cause Cons: Requires runtime font metric measurement (complex, mpv/libass doesn't expose this easily) Still fragile across font changes IMPORTANT Recommendation: Option A. Build hit zones during rendering, same as DM. This eliminates the independent re-calculation drift entirely. The dw_line_height_mul calibration constant still controls the layout model, but the hit-test will always match the render's coordinate system. Affected Code FileFunctionChange main.lua draw_dw layout loopAdd hit-zone population (like DM's draw_drum) main.lua dw_hit_testReplace independent y-geometry with zone lookup
