20260521155845 x x x x Reading anchor 20260521153302 in context with the other changes (hit-zone caching at 88d4d504, wrap-mul decoupling at 636b8fdb, edge-margin at 7c133f61), let me distill what the cumulative work actually changed about the geometric model. Before vs. after — the mechanism, not just the formula ConceptBeforeAfter Render anchorOne {\pos(960, 540)}{\an5} for the whole 15-line block, then \N/\N\N joinersOne {\pos(960, y_top)}{\an8} per visual line, joined by raw newlines Line spacing sourcelibass computed it internally from font metrics + \vspWe compute y_top ourselves; libass renders each event at the position we dictate Hit-test geometryIndependent recomputation using lh_mul=0.87 (a calibration guess for libass)Reads back the same y_top table the renderer wrote Drift after 20 visual lines~30 px (one full row)Zero, by construction Boundary behaviourFlush to y=0 / y=1080Padded by edge_margin Intra vs inter spacingBoth governed by lh_mul, tied to inter-subtitle calibrationwrap_mul for intra (descender-safe), lh_mul for inter (calibration preserved) What "more precise" actually means here The formula is not more precise in an absolute sense — lh_mul = 0.87 is still an approximation of libass's true line height for any given font. What changed is that the formula is now self-consistent: render_y(vline_k) ≡ hit_test_y(vline_k) because both sides read from the same cached FSM.DW_HIT_ZONES table built once during render. The calibration constant can be wrong vs reality and nothing drifts — render and hit-test wear the same glasses. Distilled DW geometric model vline_h = font_size · dw_wrap_line_height_mul + dw_vsp # intra-subtitle sub_gap = font_size · (dw_line_height_mul + dw_block_gap_mul) + dw_vsp # inter-subtitle entry.h = #vlines · vline_h total_h = Σ entry.h + (#layout − 1) · sub_gap raw_top = base_h/2 − offset_to_active_center block_top = clamp(raw_top, base_h − edge_margin − total_h, edge_margin) # only if total_h > base_h − 2·edge_margin y_top(e, k) = entry_y_top(e) + (k − 1) · vline_h render: {\pos(960, y_top)}{\an8} hit_zone: { y_top, y_top + vline_h } One remaining tidy-up worth flagging calculate_sub_gap still spells the inter-subtitle gap as font_size·lh_mul + font_size·block_gap_mul + vsp. That two-term split is a fossil from the \N\N rendering era — lh_mul represented "the blank line \N\N adds" and block_gap_mul represented "extra configurable gap". Now that we position every line absolutely, the gap is a single conceptual quantity: vertical advance between subtitles. The split is harmless (and the default 0.87 + (-0.27) = 0.60 works), but it's slightly misleading — anyone reading the code now will wonder why two multipliers, and tuning either one alone is unintuitive. Cleanest collapse would be: -one option instead of two dw_sub_gap_mul = 0.60, -formula sub_gap = font_size × dw_sub_gap_mul + dw_vsp This isn't a precision improvement — it's a clarity improvement. The current behavior is correct; the names just no longer match the meaning. Want me to do that cleanup as a small follow-up, or leave the calibration knobs as they are?
